+By Devin Day , Operations Officer · Reviewed by Tim Nash , Broker-in-Charge · Updated November 2, 2026
+The costliest mistake here is paying the 6 percent tax on a home you live in: about $2,930 a year on a $350,000 house. Nine more follow, by cost.
 
-## The most common Myrtle Beach buyer mistakes
+The short answer
+Of people who bought a house in Horry County in 2024 and get its tax bill at that house, about 1 in 7 are still taxed at 6 percent. That is the costliest mistake here, and it comes back every year until they apply for 4 percent.
+Flood insurance, wind cover and the seller's low tax bill come next.
 
-### Not reading the HOA rules before falling in love with the house
-Buyers tour a house, picture their life in it, and only read the HOA covenants after they are already emotionally committed. The rules can restrict short-term and long-term rentals, pets, parking, golf carts, fences, and exterior changes. The classic trap: you have spent years training your dog to walk off leash, then buy into a community with a strict leash rule and lose the off-leash walks you valued most. Read the bylaws before you make an offer, not after. Ask us and we will get you the governing documents for any community, from our own files if we already have them or by requesting them for you if we do not. It is also why a Chapter3 agent asks a lot of questions about how you live. We would rather spend the time up front than match you to a home with a rule or an HOA that blocks what you enjoy or turns out to be a poor investment.
+Found a house? We can check it for these mistakes before you offer.
+Have us check a house
 
-### Skipping the insurance quote until after the inspection period
-Most buyers get a general home inspection and fall in love with the property before ever asking what it costs to insure. In Myrtle Beach, you need three separate policies: homeowners (HO-3), wind and hail, and NFIP flood insurance. Together these can add $400 to $700 per month to your total housing cost.
-Get all three insurance quotes during the due diligence period, before you waive your right to walk away. A property that looks affordable in the listing can become a budget problem once coastal insurance is factored in.
+## What are the biggest mistakes when buying a house in Myrtle Beach?
+Each one says what it costs and when we check for it, and links to its section. If this is your first home, read our first-time buyer guide too.
 
-### Assuming an Airbnb listing means the property is legally rentable
-Short-term rentals are banned in virtually all residential zones inside Myrtle Beach city limits. Fewer than 30 homes citywide are grandfathered. If you see a property listed on Airbnb, that does not mean it is operating legally. Many listings violate current ordinances.
-If you intend to short-term rent a property, have your agent verify the specific address against current zoning maps before you make an offer. The investor chapter of this site has detailed STR zoning information by submarket.
+- 1 Paying the 6 percent tax on a home you live in About $2,930 a year more than the 4 percent rate We check: before you offer
 
-### Not reviewing the condo HOA documents before going under contract
-Condo buyers who skip the HOA document review are taking on unknown liability. Before contracting, you need to see: the HOA budget, the reserve study and funding percentage, meeting minutes from the last two years, any pending or recently levied special assessments, and whether there is active litigation against the association. If a building is in a lawsuit, read what a condo in litigation means for your financing.
-A building with a low monthly HOA fee and an underfunded reserve is a future special assessment waiting to happen. Assessments of $5,000 to $50,000 per unit have been levied in aging oceanfront buildings on the Grand Strand.
+- 2 Budgeting from the seller's tax bill About $3,400 a year over the seller's bill, for a second home We check: before you offer
 
-### Ignoring the flood zone designation until the lender requires it
-About 20% of Myrtle Beach properties fall in a FEMA-designated flood zone. Properties in Zone AE or VE require lender-mandated flood insurance, which can cost significantly more than the NFIP average. An Elevation Certificate showing the property above Base Flood Elevation can reduce premiums dramatically.
-Check the flood zone on any property at msc.fema.gov before you get emotionally invested. Reclassification through a LOMA can sometimes move a property out of a required-insurance zone, but that process takes time and money.
+- 3 No flood insurance outside the high-risk zone About 48 years of flood insurance, paid out in one flood We check: after the seller accepts your offer
 
-### Using the seller's agent without their own buyer representation
-The listing agent works for the seller. Their fiduciary duty is to the seller's best interest, not yours. An industry rule requires agents who use the shared listing service to sign a written agreement with you before a tour. State law does not require one. In South Carolina an agent represents you only after you both sign an agreement that says so. Buyers still sometimes tour with the listing agent and assume they are represented.
-Who pays your agent is written in the agreement you sign, and it is negotiable. Sellers often agree to pay it, but you may owe part or all of it. Get your own representation.
+- 4 A flood zone the listing does not mention About $1,070 a year of flood insurance the loan requires We check: before you offer
 
-### Overestimating rental income and underestimating operating costs
-Out-of-state investors often project gross Airbnb revenue without accounting for five costs. Property management fees take 20 to 30% of gross. HOA regime fees are $300 to $900 per month in oceanfront buildings. The annual hurricane deductible reserve is 2 to 5% of dwelling value. Maintenance and vacancy are the other two. A unit projecting $30,000 gross per year can easily net under $10,000 after real operating expenses.
-Run a complete cost model before you make an offer. The DSCR calculator on this site models the full expense picture including SC county property taxes.
+- 5 A homeowners policy that leaves out wind The wind damage the policy does not pay We check: before you offer
 
-### Not getting pre-approved with an SC-licensed coastal lender
-A standard pre-approval from a national or online lender does not account for the unique underwriting requirements of coastal South Carolina. HOA regime fees affect your debt-to-income ratio. Condotel financing is different from standard condo financing. DSCR loans for investment properties have specific reserve requirements. Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac have specific condo eligibility rules that disqualify some buildings entirely.
-Get pre-approved with a lender who specializes in coastal SC transactions before you start touring. It will save you from making offers on properties your financing cannot support.
+- 6 A condo building short on repair savings About $4,750 a unit in one bill, in our example We check: before you offer
 
-### Closing without understanding SC deed stamps and closing costs
-In a normal sale the seller pays South Carolina's deed recording fee, $3.70 per $1,000 of the price. The buyer pays it when buying at a master-in-equity sale or from a government seller. A buyer still pays attorney fees, title insurance, recording fees and, for a condo, an HOA transfer fee of $150 to $500 or more. South Carolina requires an attorney to conduct the closing.
-Budget 2 to 4% of purchase price for total closing costs, not including the down payment. Ask your lender for a detailed Loan Estimate within three business days of application.
