-## Short-term rental rules in the City of Myrtle Beach
-This is the single most important thing an investor needs to know about Myrtle Beach, and most get it wrong: the city prohibits short-term rentals in nearly all traditional residential neighborhoods. Every zoning district beginning with R is off-limits for rentals under 90 days, with the lone exception of the RMV (Residential Multifamily Visitor) zone, and fewer than 30 grandfathered houses exist citywide. The city defines short-term as under 90 days, the same line the state uses, and treats STRs as a commercial use. A rental needs a business license and collects 13 percent in lodging taxes and fees on the rent. See the City of Myrtle Beach short-term rental guidance . If your plan is nightly rental income, you must buy in an STR-eligible zone (tourist-commercial or RMV) or a designated resort building, not a random R-zoned house. Every jurisdiction's licenses and taxes: short-term rental rules by city .
