or="middle">54%</text><text x="537.3" y="284.0" font-family="DM Sans,sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#8a8276" text-anchor="middle">Oct</text></g><g class="bar"><rect x="572.3" y="175.3" width="31.4" height="92.7" rx="3" fill="var(--brass)"><title>Nov: 38% occupancy</title></rect><text x="588.0" y="170.3" font-family="DM Sans,sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#5a5346" text-anchor="middle">38%</text><text x="588.0" y="284.0" font-family="DM Sans,sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#8a8276" text-anchor="middle">Nov</text></g><g class="bar"><rect x="622.9" y="194.8" width="31.4" height="73.2" rx="3" fill="var(--brass)"><title>Dec: 30% occupancy</title></rect><text x="638.6" y="189.8" font-family="DM Sans,sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#5a5346" text-anchor="middle">30%</text><text x="638.6" y="284.0" font-family="DM Sans,sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#8a8276" text-anchor="middle">Dec</text></g></svg><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:.75rem;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem"><caption style="text-align:left;font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d675d;margin-bottom:.4rem">Typical short-term rental occupancy by month (illustrative)</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col" style="text-align:left;padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--navy)">Month</th><th scope="col" style="text-align:right;padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--navy)">Occupancy</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Jan</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">27%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Feb</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">30%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Mar</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">42%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Apr</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">55%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">May</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">62%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Jun</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">74%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Jul</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">80%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Aug</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">72%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Sep</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">58%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Oct</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">54%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Nov</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">38%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Dec</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">30%</td></tr></tbody></table><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.74rem;color:#6d675d;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.5rem">Illustrative Grand Strand short-term rental seasonality. Pawleys runs a lower-volume, higher-rate luxury rental model with strong golf-season shoulders.</p></figure><div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:8px;padding:1.5rem 1.6rem;margin:2rem 0"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.62rem;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:1rem">Short-term rental data &middot; Pawleys Island</p><div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(112px,1fr));gap:1.2rem 1rem;margin-bottom:1.15rem"><div><div style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.55rem;color:var(--navy);line-height:1.1">$27,953</div><div style="font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.05em;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.15rem">average annual revenue</div></div><div><div style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.55rem;color:var(--navy);line-height:1.1">33.4%</div><div style="font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.05em;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.15rem">occupancy</div></div><div><div style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.55rem;color:var(--navy);line-height:1.1">$392</div><div style="font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.05em;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.15rem">average nightly rate</div></div><div><div style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.55rem;color:var(--navy);line-height:1.1">428</div><div style="font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.05em;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.15rem">active listings</div></div></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.87rem;line-height:1.65;margin:0">The strongest tenth of listings here gross $7,499 a month or more; the median listing makes $2,528. June is the strongest month and January the weakest. Averages cover every listing in the market, including part-time ones, so a well-run property can sit well above them. <a href="/invest/airbnb-income/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Compare all nine submarkets</a>. <span style="font-size:.78rem">AirROI, data through 2026-08-08.</span></p></div><h2>How rental returns work in Pawleys Island</h2><p>Luxury rentals earn differently. Occupancy is lower than in the high-volume beach towns, but weekly rates on a well-positioned beach house are far higher, and the buyer is often as motivated by appreciation and personal use as by yield. Estimate a realistic luxury occupancy, account for the heavier upkeep of an older high-end home, and treat strong appreciation potential as part of the total return rather than relying on cash flow alone.</p><h2>Financing an investment property in Pawleys Island</h2><p>Higher price points mean jumbo financing enters the picture on many island and luxury properties, with stricter reserve and documentation requirements. Second-home loans are common given the buyer profile. For income properties, DSCR financing works but the lower occupancy of a luxury rental affects the coverage calculation, so the estimate has to be realistic about achievable rents.</p><h2>Pawleys Island rules and costs that move the numbers</h2><p>At Pawleys price points, the cost lines carry weight. South Carolina's 6 percent assessment ratio on non-owner-occupied property, applied to a higher-value home, produces a substantial tax bill, and Chapter3 will confirm it early. Oceanfront and creek-side properties on or near the island need serious wind and flood coverage, which is a recurring cost worth quoting before you offer. Short-term luxury rentals also remit state and local accommodations taxes. On an appreciation-driven luxury asset, these carrying costs are the difference between a comfortable hold and a strained one.</p><h2>The Pawleys Island investor thesis</h2><ol class="thesis-list"><li><span class="thesis-num">01</span><span class="thesis-text">This is a scarcity-and-luxury play: limited supply and protected character support values over time.</span></li><li><span class="thesis-num">02</span><span class="thesis-text">Premium weekly rates on well-positioned beach houses can outweigh lower occupancy in a luxury model.</span></li><li><span class="thesis-num">03</span><span class="thesis-text">Litchfield and the Caledonia and True Blue golf communities add golf-trip rental demand and a deep second-home buyer pool.</span></li><li><span class="thesis-num">04</span><span class="thesis-text">Patience pays. The right asset is worth waiting for in a thin-inventory market.</span></li></ol><h2>What a first Pawleys Island deal looks like</h2><p>A first Pawleys investment is rarely an entry-level move, so the realistic version is often a Litchfield or golf-community home rather than oceanfront on the island itself. These carry more attainable price points while still drawing golf-trip rental demand and a deep second-home buyer pool. Estimate luxury-market occupancy, budget honestly for the upkeep of an older or higher-end home, and treat appreciation and personal use as part of the return. Patience matters here: thin inventory means the right asset is worth waiting for.</p><h2>What to watch before you buy in Pawleys Island</h2><ul style="padding-left:1.1rem;margin-top:.5rem"><li style="margin-bottom:.7rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7"><strong>High entry price.</strong> Capital requirements are well above the northern Strand. Returns depend on appreciation and premium rates, not volume.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.7rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7"><strong>Older-home maintenance.</strong> Classic beach houses carry real upkeep. Inspect thoroughly and budget for it.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.7rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7"><strong>Town versus county rules.</strong> The barrier island and the mainland follow different jurisdictions and rental rules. Confirm for the exact address.</li></ul><h2>Where Pawleys Island sits, and how it is governed</h2><p>Pawleys Island is a Georgetown County market about 25 miles south of Myrtle Beach and one of the oldest summer resorts on the East Coast, known for its understated "arrogantly shabby" character. It has two parts that matter for buyers. One is the small incorporated Town of Pawleys Island, which is the barrier island itself, a tiny town. The other is the larger unincorporated mainland, which includes Litchfield, Pawleys Plantation, Hagley Estates, The River Club, and, to the south, gated DeBordieu Colony.</p><h2>The Pawleys Island market right now</h2><p>Pawleys Island is the upscale end of the southern Strand. Zillow's home-value index was near $514,000 in mid-2026, up about 5 percent year over year. Other trackers put the median list price in the mid $500,000s. Waterfront and gated-community homes are priced well higher, with DeBordieu reaching into the multi-millions. See the <a href="https://www.zillow.com/home-values/35932/pawleys-island-sc/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Zillow Pawleys Island home values</a>. Small-sample monthly medians can swing hard here, so treat any single month with caution and price by community. <!-- TIMMY: current CCAR MLS median and DOM for Pawleys/Litchfield by community. --></p><h2>Short-term rental rules in Pawleys Island</h2><p>Short-term rentals are generally allowed in the Pawleys Island area, and there is no permanent ban; the only documented prohibition was a temporary COVID-era emergency ordinance in 2020. Rules differ by jurisdiction: the incorporated Town of Pawleys Island governs the barrier island, while mainland communities fall under Georgetown County zoning. See <a href="https://www.gtcounty.org/179/Zoning" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Georgetown County zoning</a>. On top of that, gated communities like Pawleys Plantation and DeBordieu have their own governing documents that can restrict rentals, and Chapter3 will verify both the jurisdiction and the HOA for a specific address. <!-- TIMMY: which Pawleys/Litchfield communities restrict rentals, and STR vs LTR performance by area. --> Every jurisdiction's licenses and taxes: <a href="/invest/str-rules/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">short-term rental rules by city</a>.</p><h2>Owning in Pawleys Island: the 4 percent versus 6 percent property tax rule</h2><p>South Carolina assesses an owner-occupied primary residence at a 4 percent ratio, and it does not pay the school operating tax. A second home or investment is assessed at 6 percent and pays the school operating tax. On Georgetown County's 2025 rates, a second home's tax bill on Pawleys Island is about 3.1 times a primary home's on the same house. Georgetown County's combined sales tax is 7 percent. See the <a href="https://dor.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">South Carolina Department of Revenue</a> for the assessment-ratio rules. Full county math, with a calculator: <a href="/buyers/property-taxes/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Horry County property taxes</a>.</p><h2>Flood zones and insurance in Pawleys Island</h2><p>Flood exposure is high and central to buying here, because much of the market is on or near a barrier island and tidal creeks. Public First Street data reported by Redfin flags an extreme share of Pawleys Island properties (around 89 percent) as facing severe flood risk over 30 years. Nearly the entire area carries elevated wind risk. Flood insurance and elevation certificates are not optional details here; get them for the specific address early. <!-- TIMMY: which streets and sections flood, and current flood/wind premium ranges by community. --> What the three coastal policies cost: <a href="/buyers/coastal-insurance/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">coastal home insurance costs</a>.</p><h2>Pawleys Island lifestyle and amenities</h2><p>Pawleys Island trades nightlife for nature and history: quiet beaches, salt-marsh creeks, surfing and sea-turtle nesting, a historic district dating to the 1700s, and Jack Nicklaus-designed golf at Pawleys Plantation. Brookgreen Gardens and Huntington Beach State Park are just north, dining and shopping run along the Ocean Highway (U.S. 17) on the mainland, and the pace is deliberately slower than the rest of the Strand.</p><h2>Schools serving Pawleys Island</h2><p>Public schools fall within the Georgetown County School District, generally the Waccamaw schools for the Litchfield and Pawleys area. Those are Waccamaw Elementary, Waccamaw Intermediate, Waccamaw Middle, and Waccamaw High. Private options include Lowcountry Preparatory School. Assignment is by address, and Chapter3 will confirm the exact schools on the district boundary map.</p><h2>What it is like to live in Pawleys Island</h2><p>Pawleys Island trades nightlife for nature and history: quiet beaches, salt-marsh creeks, a historic district dating to the 1700s, and an understated, "arrogantly shabby" character. Brookgreen Gardens and Huntington Beach State Park are just north, dining and shopping run along Ocean Highway on the mainland, and the pace is deliberately slower than the rest of the Strand.</p><p>For a live-in buyer, flood is central: much of the market is on or near a barrier island and tidal creeks, so flood insurance and an elevation certificate are essential for any purchase. Georgetown County property tax runs low relative to the national average, and the area skews toward higher-end, gated, and golf communities.</p><h2>Buying in Pawleys Island as a primary residence</h2><p>Pawleys is the upscale, lifestyle end of the southern Strand, and many buyers here want a primary residence or a quiet second home rather than a rental. It suits patient, lifestyle-driven buyers who want understated and established over entertainment and volume, including luxury buyers in gated communities like Pawleys Plantation or DeBordieu. A primary residence is taxed at 4% with the owner-occupant credit; a second home at 6% without it.</p><h2>Read the Pawleys Island HOA rules before you commit to a house</h2><p>In a market with this many associations, the covenants matter as much as the house. Before you make an offer, read the HOA bylaws and rules, because they can restrict short-term and long-term rentals, pets, parking, golf carts, fences, and exterior changes. The classic trap: you have spent years training your dog to walk off leash, then buy into a community with a strict leash rule and lose the thing you valued most. Read the documents before you make an offer, not after.</p><p>Ask us and we will get you the governing documents for any Pawleys Island community, from our own files if we already have them or by requesting them for you if we do not. It is also why a Chapter3 agent asks a lot of questions about how you live. We would rather spend the time up front than match you to a home with a rule or an HOA that blocks what you enjoy or turns out to be a poor investment.</p><h2>Pawleys Island community stats</h2><p>Official figures from government sources, with no interpretation added. Each links to its source so you can read the full data yourself.</p><div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:12px;padding:1.25rem 1.4rem;margin:1rem 0"><p style="font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.5rem">Population &amp; income &middot; <a href="https://www.census.gov/quickfacts/fact/table/georgetowncountysouthcarolina" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Census Bureau</a></p><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Town of Pawleys Island population (2020 Census)</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">about 130</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Median household income (Georgetown County, ACS)</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right"><a href="https://www.census.gov/quickfacts/fact/table/georgetowncountysouthcarolina" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Census Bureau</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Note</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">the market is mostly unincorporated Georgetown County mainland</span></div><p style="font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin:1.25rem 0 .5rem">Cost, taxes &amp; risk &middot; government sources</p><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Median home value (2024 estimate)</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">about $620,000</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Homeownership rate</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">about 80%</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Property assessment ratio</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">4% primary / 6% second home &nbsp;<a href="https://dor.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC DOR</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Flood hazard by address</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right"><a href="https://msc.fema.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FEMA Flood Map Service Center</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Crime statistics</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right"><a href="https://cde.ucr.cjis.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FBI Crime Data Explorer</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.sled.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SLED</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">School performance</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right"><a href="https://screportcards.ed.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Dept. of Education report cards</a></span></div></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.7">How to pull the crime numbers yourself: on the <a href="https://cde.ucr.cjis.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FBI Crime Data Explorer</a>, choose South Carolina, then select the local agency to see reported offenses by year. The local agency is the county sheriff's office or the city police department. On <a href="https://www.sled.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SLED</a>, open the current "Crime in South Carolina" report and find the county and agency tables. Chapter3 will pull the current figures for a specific address with you.</p><h2>Pawleys Island school stats</h2><p>Pawleys Island and Litchfield are served by the Georgetown County School District, generally the Waccamaw schools. Figures are from the South Carolina Department of Education report card and federal education data, with no interpretation added.</p><div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:12px;padding:1.25rem 1.4rem;margin:1rem 0"><p style="font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.5rem">Waccamaw High School &middot; <a href="https://screportcards.ed.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC report card</a></p><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Grades served</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">9-12</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Enrollment</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">about 846</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Four-year graduation rate</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">about 94% (state average about 84%)</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Reading and writing proficiency</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">about 90% (state average 52%)</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Math proficiency</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right"><a href="https://screportcards.ed.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">see report card</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Student-to-teacher ratio</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">about 14 to 1</span></div></div><p>For the elementary and middle schools that feed it, and for any exact address, read the full report cards at the <a href="https://screportcards.ed.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">South Carolina Department of Education</a>. The report card also publishes SAT and ACT averages, Advanced Placement and dual-enrollment participation, college-and-career readiness, dropout rate, and chronic absenteeism. Ask your Chapter3 agent and we will pull the report card for the school an address is zoned to.</p><h2>Who Pawleys Island suits</h2><p>Pawleys Island fits second-home and primary buyers who want an upscale, quiet, natural coastal setting and are comfortable with barrier-island flood and insurance realities, along with luxury buyers targeting gated communities like Pawleys Plantation or DeBordieu. It suits patient, lifestyle-driven buyers more than yield-chasing investors, though waterfront rentals can perform. If you want entertainment and volume, the northern Strand fits better; if you want understated and established, this is the place.</p></div>
